question.:question/worked-solution:
Consider each coordinate separately.

For $x$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
x &=- t^{2} + 2 t - 1 \\ &=- \left(t - 1\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The vertex parameter $t=1$ is the domain boundary, which is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $x\le 0$.

For $y$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y &=- 2 t^{2} + 4 t - 3 \\ &=- 2 \left(t - 1\right)^{2} - 1
\end{aligned}
$$

The vertex parameter $t=1$ is the domain boundary, which is included. The coordinate is continuous on the domain and unbounded below, so every value on the stated side of the bound occurs. Thus $y\le -1$.

Therefore, $x\le 0,\,y\le -1$.
